6,11,1147,62,17,11,1124,507,2412,11,1147,1710,3,387,279,12460,17516,315,400,47,2075,15087,11,323,1077,400,89,4698,61,17,24239,4698,35093,15159,74,92,72,3,369,400,74,28,16,11,220,17,11,1124,507,2412,11,435,54876,1380,400,72,34433,26888,19999,16,31716,11,323,400,64,4698,3,323,400,65,4698,3,525,1931,5109,13,7379,198,59,26056,1242,15159,74,28,16,92,47822,81,52398,65,4698,91,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $$P(x)=24x^{24}+\\sum_{j=1}^{23}(24-j)\\left(x^{24-j}+x^{24+j}\\right).$$Let $z_1, z_2, \\ldots, z_r$ be the distinct zeros of $P(x)$, and let $z_k^2=a_k+b_{k}i$ for $k=1, 2, \\ldots, r$, where $i=\\sqrt{-1}$, and $a_k$ and $b_k$ are real numbers. Find\n\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{r}|b_k|.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6e59f3abed445e65fd2d8bc0a6d3555b1672293de030c3fa08c20b4900627ecf"},"seconds":54.37048125267029,"seed":455421109883385425,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we start by analyzing the polynomial \\( P(x) \\):\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\left( x^{24-j} + x^{24+j} \\right).\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's expand and simplify \\( P(x) \\):\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24-j} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24+j}.\n\\]\n\nNotice that the polynomial \\( P(x) \\) is symmetric about \\( x^{24} \\). To further understand the polynomial, consider the substitution \\( y = x + \\frac{1}{x} \\). We know that:\n\\[\nx^n + x^{-n} = T_n(y),\n\\]\nwhere \\( T_n(y) \\) are the Chebyshev polynomials of the first kind.\n\nGiven the symmetry and structure of \\( P(x) \\), we can rewrite it in terms of \\( y \\):\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\left( x^{24-j} + x^{24+j} \\right).\n\\]\n\nBy substituting \\( x = e^{i\\theta} \\), we get:\n\\[\nP(e^{i\\theta}) = 24 e^{24i\\theta} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\left( e^{i(24-j)\\theta} + e^{i(24+j)\\theta} \\right).\n\\]\n\nUsing Euler's formula \\( e^{i\\theta} + e^{-i\\theta} = 2 \\cos \\theta \\), we can simplify each term:\n\\[\ne^{i(24-j)\\theta} + e^{i(24+j)\\theta} = 2 \\cos((24-j)\\theta).\n\\]\n\nThus, the polynomial becomes:\n\\[\nP(e^{i\\theta}) = 24 \\cos(24\\theta) + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\left( 2 \\cos((24-j)\\theta) \\right).\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\nP(e^{i\\theta}) = 24 \\cos(24\\theta) + 2 \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\cos((24-j)\\theta).\n\\]\n\nSince \\( P(x) \\) is a polynomial of degree 47, it has 48 roots in the complex plane. The roots \\( z_k \\) are symmetric about the unit circle. Given the symmetry and the fact that the polynomial is real, the roots come in conjugate pairs.\n\nThe roots of \\( P(x) \\) are of the form \\( e^{i\\theta_k} \\), and their squares are:\n\\[\nz_k^2 = e^{2i\\theta_k} = \\cos(2\\theta_k) + i \\sin(2\\theta_k).\n\\]\n\nThe imaginary parts of \\( z_k^2 \\) are \\( \\sin(2\\theta_k) \\). The sum of the absolute values of these imaginary parts is:\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{r} |b_k| = \\sum_{k=1}^{24} |\\sin(2\\theta_k)|.\n\\]\n\nSince the roots are symmetric and cover the unit circle, each \\( \\sin(2\\theta_k) \\) will appear in pairs with opposite signs. Therefore, the sum of the absolute values is:\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{24} |\\sin(2\\theta_k)| = 24.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{24}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{24}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
